# band-boxes.rq — which text bands hold >= 2 closed ruled boxes (spec
# 2026-09-28-box-split-design.md § 3.2, ruling R-d).
#
# Open-world derivation over a per-call evidence graph (the closure boundary is one fresh
# Graph() per boxsplit.box_evidence call, the same shape as band-run.rq/section-repeat.rq/
# grid-donation.rq close within their own transient graphs): one tab:ClosedBox node per
# closed ruled box that BELONGS to a band (I-2a — every page word inside the box's bbox is a
# word of that band, computed by boxsplit._box_owner and carried as tab:boxBandIndex). A box
# with no words, or whose words span more than one band, belongs to none and emits NOTHING —
# the honest abstain sectiongraph.run_evidence already makes for its own population — so it
# can never be counted here in either direction.
#
# THE COUNT CLOSES WITHIN THE BAND. GROUP BY groups the evidence by the band it belongs to,
# and HAVING counts only that one group's own members — holon-scoped, the same idiom
# band-run.rq's query-local NOT EXISTS uses to close within one band while the graph stays
# open (CLAUDE.md §8).
#
# THE ONLY NUMERIC LITERAL IS 2 (I-2b), and it is not a tuned threshold: ruling R-d defines
# "this band holds more than one table" AS "this band holds at least two closed boxes" — two
# is the smallest count "more than one" admits, not a measured cutoff, and no other numeral
# could stand in its place without changing what the ruling means.
#
# THIS DERIVATION ENUMERATES AND SETTLES NOTHING FURTHER: it names which band indices split.
# boxsplit.bands_to_split re-derives, in Python, which boxes each selected band owns (the same
# I-2a membership test the emitter already ran) to assemble the answer; Task 3's word
# partition and band construction are what actually performs the split.
PREFIX tab: <https://w3id.org/iladub/tab#>
SELECT ?band WHERE {
  ?box a tab:ClosedBox ; tab:boxBandIndex ?band .
}
GROUP BY ?band
HAVING (COUNT(?box) >= 2)
